00:02:00.795 --> 00:02:02.433
Hittin' the weights.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.755
Okay, so number one question, Gary.

00:02:05.755 --> 00:02:06.588
- Yes.

00:02:06.588 --> 00:02:07.870
- We got a lot of high schools in here.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.730
And I want to know, we want to know,

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.290
what were you like in high school, man?

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.270
What was the Gary V at that time

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.200
before this Gary V arrived?

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.180
- So, it's funny.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.342
This is interesting timing.

00:02:18.342 --> 00:02:21.830
For only a week or two ago I posted my

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.380
high school report card on Instagram.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.580
Because I was starting to realize

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.060
through a lot of the feedback

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.057
that people that were consuming my content

00:02:31.057 --> 00:02:32.920
didn't realize when I was saying

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.330
that I was a D and F
student in high school,

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.240
that I was a D and F student.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.490
I would see a lot of people
reference B's and C's,

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.520
which I guess, to
straight A student culture

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.253
seems like D's and F's.

00:02:46.162 --> 00:02:48.840
I had a very unique high school career.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.190
Number one, when I started high school,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.300
the summer before in eighth grade

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.270
was when my Dad dragged
me into the liquor store.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:01.330
And I basically stopped
hanging out with friends

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.330
in eighth grade.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.420
So every summer day of that vacation

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.320
leading up to high school,

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.960
and every weekend in high school, I worked

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:09.910
at the store.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.660
And I mean, just so everybody knows,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.950
not checking the box,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.480
like you want your kid to work.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.040
More like, because I'm
first generation immigrant.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.890
More like, the merchants
of the '50s and '60s.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:25.020
Meaning, on Saturday,
I left my house at 7am,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.770
and we got home at 9pm.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:30.430
And on Sunday we left at
10am and got home at 6.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.550
Just worked.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.610
And so that's number one, number two,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.636
I am unbelievably, I see a lot of faces,

00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:39.540
a lot of people have some context for me.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.908
I am unbelievably not comfortable

00:03:41.908 --> 00:03:44.640
being mean to another person.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.160
Just completely does
not come natural to me.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.520
I hate negativity.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.080
I hate cynicism.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.410
And I definitely hate suppressing somebody

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:53.770
or hurting their feelings.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.830
So I was very awkward for high school

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.800
where that is of culture,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.450
and so I was awfully friendly
with a lot of people.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.602
It wasn't that I was so
popular with everybody,

00:04:03.602 --> 00:04:05.470
it's just that I really genuinely

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.320
wanted to be friends with everybody.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.890
Third, I was trying to sell so much shit

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.710
to my friends in high school.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.650
Like I would literally take all of lunch

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.250
freshman and sophomore year,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.110
and literally walk table to table

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.333
trying to sell people baseball cards.

00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:23.996
Which wasn't necessarily the
coolest fuckin' thing to do,

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:27.060
but very much kind of where I was

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.660
as a person.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.330
And then finally, I
would say the last thing

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.050
that sums me up in high school was

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.660
we had a Senior breakfast.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:34.493
I don't know,

00:04:34.493 --> 00:04:36.060
I never really heard of
this but our high school

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.610
thought it was a tradition.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.870
Somewhere in March or April with

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.780
maybe a month or two left in high school,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.270
the whole Senior class got together

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.560
in the cafeteria and they
did a nice breakfast.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.210
And then they gave out mics
for people to say things.

00:04:49.147 --> 00:04:51.470
And not a lot of people
wanted to say things

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:52.810
or sappy things.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.170
And I'll never forget, my one friend

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.619
took the mic and he goes,

00:04:55.619 --> 00:04:58.747
"the number one thing I'll
remember about high school

00:04:58.747 --> 00:05:01.837
"is always being super
hopeful that Gary Vaynerchuk

00:05:01.837 --> 00:05:04.028
"was in my class, 'cause I knew that meant

00:05:04.028 --> 00:05:06.667
"that he was gonna distract the teacher

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:09.447
"from between seven and 15 minutes a day

00:05:09.447 --> 00:05:11.317
"and we would end up not working as hard

00:05:11.317 --> 00:05:12.275
"in that class."

00:05:12.275 --> 00:05:13.380
(audience laughs)

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.213
So that was me.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:16.400
- So, any of those people in this room

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.840
that distracter?

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:18.700
Yeah.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.840
Alright, so I have more questions,

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.033
I will fill in gaps as needed.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.670
But this is about everyone
in here in the room,

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.419
in particular, the students, so

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:32.373
one thing, one quick rule.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.010
Quick questions, maybe
not too many follow ups.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:38.691
And we'll keep it flowin'
so everyone can kind of

00:05:38.691 --> 00:05:41.311
get in as much as possible
while Gary is with us, right?

00:05:41.311 --> 00:05:42.743
- You got the mic over there?

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.985
- The microphone's right over there,

00:05:46.985 --> 00:05:50.500
if you want to walk over so we
can hear you loud and clear.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.300
Maybe as that first brave
person's walking over,

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.450
just maybe hello, your name, and

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:58.730
I know most of the questions are gonna be

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:00.400
directed for me but I really encourage you

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.312
to ask Gary.

00:06:01.312 --> 00:06:03.400
(laughs)

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.233
- Hey man.

00:06:04.233 --> 00:06:05.066
- [Martin] Hey Gary, big fan.

00:06:05.066 --> 00:06:06.150
- Thank you, what's your name?

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:06.983
- [Martin] Martin.

00:06:06.983 --> 00:06:07.816
- Martin
- [Martin] Martin,

00:06:07.816 --> 00:06:08.755
on the associate board like

00:06:08.755 --> 00:06:09.929
a couple of these people are here.

00:06:09.929 --> 00:06:12.760
I listen to your podcast
and one of the themes

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.930
that I get is that you've taught people

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:15.897
that you wouldn't be where you are today

00:06:15.897 --> 00:06:19.350
unless you made big, bold moves
to put yourself out there.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.100
And you see what happens.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.440
And so, this is for the first time,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.100
a lot of the students in the room here

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.270
are trying that out in the program.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.230
And so sometimes they
don't get what they want

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:30.430
after they put themselves out.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:32.180
So, I wanted to ask you,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:33.640
can you tell us about a time when you

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.330
put yourself out there,

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.390
and didn't get what you wanted,

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:38.690
and how you dealt with it?

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.280
- That's a good question.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.033
Jesus.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.510
The reason I said Jesus is

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.230
it's unbelievable how much I struggle

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.510
with recalling failures,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.523
even though I have failures every day.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.360
And so I'm trying to be
empathetic to the students

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.040
and think about something
that would bring them value.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.136
Here's my thing.

00:07:01.136 --> 00:07:02.560
I'm gonna take your question,

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.370
and I'm gonna try to provide value,

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.710
which is the following.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:10.243
I'm curious who gets to own a loss.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.940
I'm curious, when I put myself out there,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.984
the biggest mistakes
I've made in my career,

00:07:16.984 --> 00:07:20.060
hands down, have been
the things I didn't do,

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.330
versus what I've done.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.100
I'm an old school immigrant.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.860
I seem aggressive in
putting myself out there,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.130
I'm ridiculously practical.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.310
I've never put myself
in any kind of position

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:32.703
that has ever made me super vulnerable

00:07:32.703 --> 00:07:34.240
to lose it.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.323
I've never bet it all, right?

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:40.179
We took a huge mortgage
in building Wine Library.

00:07:40.179 --> 00:07:42.190
That was kind of betting it all,

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:44.380
but I was, as long as I was healthy,

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.510
I was running that business.

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:48.500
To me the bigger question here is,

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:51.180
when you fail, when you do a start up,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.986
and you fail, right?

00:07:52.986 --> 00:07:55.920
Who gets to own that loss?

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.530
Is the much more interesting question that

00:07:57.530 --> 00:07:58.900
I would tell all the kids here,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:00.820
which is when you fail, when you do this

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.590
and nobody buys your hats,

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:03.540
who owns that?

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:04.910
I think I own it.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:07.570
My L's are my L's.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:11.830
Your two cents on my losses
don't mean shit to me.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.640
And I think the biggest
reason people struggle

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.400
with doing things, is they
allow somebody else's opinion

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.290
matter more than their
own about themselves.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:25.290
And so when I curse, or when
I have made bold predictions

00:08:26.119 --> 00:08:28.240
or all the things that I've done

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.460
some of the, all those fancy
people at those conferences

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.320
that I met in 2000 when
I started VaynerMedia,

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.710
they called me and said they
were disappointed in me.

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:39.860
This is real life.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.250
That whole crew of all the founders

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.010
who built trillion dollar companies

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.230
who would listen to me and
thought I was thoughtful

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.640
a decade ago,

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.530
called me when I started
VaynerMedia and said that

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.770
they were disappointed
in me because I could do

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.050
so much more than just build an agency.

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.950
I respected that because
these were the characters

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.770
that were building Facebook and Dropbox

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.810
and Uber, and I had empathy!

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:04.680
But I also have self-awareness.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.170
I wasn't gonna build
one of those companies.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.420
It's not how I'm wired, it's
not how I create wealth,

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:13.830
I knew that I was gonna eat
crow for a decade or two,

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.623
build a platform foundation
and then I would buy companies

00:09:16.623 --> 00:09:20.270
and run them through it, and
that over a 30 year period,

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.336
I could create entrepreneurial success and

00:09:22.336 --> 00:09:24.100
massive building growing.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.850
So when you have the fanciest, hottest,

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:29.300
I literally at the time had three people

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.600
on the covers of Forbes
and Ink and Fast Company

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.510
call me and say, you
basically, in a nice way,

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.040
you suck.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:40.330
And for me, I just loved
it because I couldn't wait.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:42.100
And by the way, I just mentioned three

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:43.240
that went on to crush it.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:45.830
I had eight or nine others
who raised 40 million

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:48.510
for their fancy products and they lost.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:49.840
And they have jobs now.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.942
And so, you don't know who's going to

00:09:51.942 --> 00:09:53.370
do what.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.630
My big thing is, for
the kids in this crowd,

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.060
when you do something,

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.520
when you go back to school
and nobody bought your hats

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:03.200
and they make fun of you,
I just don't understand

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.350
what the value of their opinion is.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.930
Because I know that you got
the experience of understanding

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:10.343
why did they or not buy the hats.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.682
- Love it, thank you.

00:10:15.682 --> 00:10:17.700
(applauding)
Yeah we'll take some applause.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:18.603
That's great.

00:10:18.603 --> 00:10:21.770
(audience applauding)

00:10:23.926 --> 00:10:25.423
You got a question?

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:27.823
Yeah, he's ready.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:30.229
- My name is

00:10:30.229 --> 00:10:32.290
and I was wondering, how do you stand out

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.713
in a society saturated with media?

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.440
- I think it's depth.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.120
I think it's quality.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.790
People always ask me how do I stand out,

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:45.250
how do I grow awareness,
how do I get more followers,

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:47.010
all the cliche stuff.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:50.930
It's always gonna come
down to having the chops.

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.737
I was just being interviewed
before I went up here

00:10:52.737 --> 00:10:54.030
and the young man says,

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.250
how do I get to be where you are, quickly?

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.269
And I said to him, you don't.

00:10:59.269 --> 00:11:00.350
(laughs)

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:03.280
I think the thing that is
often forgotten in my narrative

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.860
is I did work for 15
years building businesses

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.760
before I ever spoke about it

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.060
or wrote a book or gave a speech.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:14.210
And I think right now, I don't
look down on the youngsters

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:16.510
trying to build a brand or stand out

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:18.360
because I'm sure if I had the internet

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.330
at this stage when I was 16,

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.270
I would have gravitated
towards it with my personality,

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:24.280
I have empathy.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.860
The problem is, the only way to stand out,

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:30.390
in my opinion, is to stand
out for the long term.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:32.570
You can do a lot of things to stand out

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:34.020
for the next week.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.580
You can use shock movement,

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:38.240
you can growth hack,
you can buy followers,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.750
all the dumb shit that everybody's doing

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.550
to win for a week or a
month, maybe even a year.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.060
To me, the question is how
do you stand out forever?

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.490
And that's being a tortoise, not a hare.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.650
That's doing the right
thing day in and day out.

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:54.092
That's deploying patience, and recognizing

00:11:54.092 --> 00:11:57.240
the only people that you
want to be that stand out

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.280
are the ones that are
doing something of depth

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.130
and that takes a long time to create.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.700
And so that, I think
about standing out from

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.550
one simple standpoint
which is having skills,

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.000
and executing against them.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.160
The thing you at 15 and
the people here at 70

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:15.520
still have 30, 40 years to go,

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:17.700
they need to figure out self-awareness.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.560
Standing out is a byproduct of you being

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.800
self aware and understanding
what you're good at

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.610
and what you love and that paradigm,

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.380
and then deploying
patience on the execution

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:28.500
of that delta.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.881
That's how you stand out.

00:12:30.881 --> 00:12:32.969
- And Gary, when you
mentioned earlier with the

00:12:32.969 --> 00:12:36.994
agency model, did you know at that,

00:12:36.994 --> 00:12:39.180
it makes perfect sense.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.090
You have a platform,
you build on top of it.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:44.030
Did you know the path at that time?

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:45.530
Or did you at the time know,

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:47.320
hey I'm gonna disrupt agencies, obviously

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.440
you've been in the game.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:50.730
The codes are ripe for disruption.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:51.700
- I knew at that time.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.880
I knew, you know what happened
during those summer nights

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.480
in Vegas or other conferences I went to

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.380
where I was spending a
lot of time with special

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.490
kids that I thought were
gonna change the world

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:02.870
and clearly have?

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:05.100
I eat my own dog food,

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:06.940
I was deploying self awareness.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.040
And after a little while
of spending time with

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.780
special people, I said, you know what?

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:14.170
What makes them special,
that doesn't make me special?

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:16.340
What makes me special that
doesn't make them special?

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:20.500
I realized at some point
that my ability to understand

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:23.690
human psychology and human nature

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.610
and what they would pay
attention to in the future

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.470
was something I was trading on.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.660
I understood consumer behavior

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:33.410
a hair of a second faster
than a lot of people

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.470
and I had the humility to put in the work

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:39.030
in new places, taste them, and
then be able to trade on it.

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:42.690
So basically what VaynerMedia
is and was at the time

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.170
was I going to build the scalable version

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.570
of my core one unique strength,

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:50.133
which is communicating in this new world.

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:53.680
- And just to say the way I heard is

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.290
you knew your superpower early.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.980
And everyone here has a superpower, right?

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:00.710
- Yeah and everybody's
superpowers are different, right?

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:03.233
Spider-Man's way cooler than Ant-Man.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.760
- There isn't an Ant-Man, is there?

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.275
- Yeah there's an Ant-Man!

00:14:08.275 --> 00:14:09.427
Dude, what the fuck?

00:14:09.427 --> 00:14:10.890
(audience laughs)

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:14.527
And so, to me, the reason I said that is

00:14:14.527 --> 00:14:18.080
I don't think everybody's destined to

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.900
build a trillion dollar company and

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.180
change the world.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.190
And by the way, nor do I think
most people aspire to it,

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:26.880
especially when they
get the taste of being

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:28.613
the last line of defense.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:32.140
You said something earlier,
we need more entrepreneurship.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.150
I would argue at this
moment, right this second,

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:35.440
we need less entrepreneurship.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:36.740
And I know that's a curveball.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.540
We need more self awareness.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:40.070
Everybody thinks they're a founder.

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:41.470
There's a whole lot of executives,

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:42.900
there's a whole lot of people that are

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.130
leaving a lot of happiness in their lives

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:46.560
because they're graduating from Morton

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.390
and think they're a founder.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:49.860
But instead what they would have been

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.400
is a great number three
or a great number seven.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:53.370
Please do not

00:14:54.603 --> 00:14:56.530
please excuse me, don't
misunderstand this.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:58.500
The makeup of a number one,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.170
a person who lives their life every day

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:03.310
knowing they're the last line of defense,

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:05.720
and has to have a constant running energy

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:08.050
in the back of their mind
of anxiety of knowing

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.810
they are the last line of defense.

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:12.780
And that, you know
what's great about school

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:14.020
and working at some

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:14.853
working somewhere,

00:15:14.853 --> 00:15:18.110
you can blame someone else if it fails!

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.130
When you start your own
company, when it fails,

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:23.028
it's a hundred percent your fault.

00:15:23.028 --> 00:15:24.490
A hundred.

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:25.870
You hired that person.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.320
You made that strategy
during this economic time.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.080
It is 100 percent your fault.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.660
And so, I think we need
more self awareness of

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:33.880
who you are.

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.423
And that is something that
I very much believe in.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:38.193
- Thank you, Gary.

00:15:38.193 --> 00:15:39.420
Let's get the next question over there,

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.200
microphone in the middle.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:42.930
And in case you guys are wondering,

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:44.183
right there in the middle.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.320
- [Gary] We'll get him next.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:49.400
- You mentioned empathy, empathy,

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:50.660
empathy.
- [Gary] Yes.

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.603
- There's very few businessmen like you,

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:58.420
your success that it
mentioned empathy so often.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:00.570
Why is it, where does it come from?

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:04.483
And why is that part of your mojo?

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.650
- I think it's gonna be
something in five to seven years

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:11.220
you'll hear most business
people talk about.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.120
I think empathy for me speaks about

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:14.953
a lot of things.

00:16:14.953 --> 00:16:17.810
I think it comes from
my mother, to be frank.

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:21.163
I think DNA-wise, and
she also nourished it.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:25.050
Early on in my life,

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.530
I showed a lot of compassion to others.

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:28.610
Back to how I mentioned earlier,

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:30.860
I don't like when people are upset.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.700
So, my Mom would

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.550
she would be very aggressive
in making a big deal

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:39.860
when I was kind or
empathetic or compassionate.

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:43.470
Later, I got to spend a
lot of time with my dad.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.050
We lived 45 minutes away
from the liquor store.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:49.734
So we would drive up Route
78 in Jersey back and forth.

00:16:49.734 --> 00:16:52.000
My dad doesn't talk.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.021
He's just an old school, eastern European,

00:16:54.021 --> 00:16:56.440
we are really different.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:59.052
Which meant I had to learn,

00:16:59.052 --> 00:17:02.640
I basically talked to my dad constantly,

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:05.070
to see how he would react,

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:08.090
even with an eyebrow or just one word.

00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:11.500
And I started really learning
how to have that relationship.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:14.010
So I think the circumstances of my parents

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.720
created a very empathetic thing for me

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.500
which then worked well
for me as a salesman

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:20.870
and as a manager,

00:17:20.870 --> 00:17:23.244
because my default in every situation.

00:17:23.244 --> 00:17:25.420
It's why I'm a good public speaker.

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:27.283
I only think about the other party.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:30.910
Because if you only think
about the other party,

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.560
the things that you worry
about will be there.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.640
If you're the more valuable
entity in the relationship,

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:38.290
it's the leverage.

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:41.740
And so I think empathy is
actually fundamentally,

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:44.790
I would argue that Steve
Jobs' design capabilities

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.900
were far more empathetic
than anything else.

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:51.340
He understood the consumer's
wants and needs, intuitively.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:54.180
I think a great leader is only empathetic.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:57.680
So, I think that's where it comes from,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:59.244
and I am very convinced,

00:17:59.244 --> 00:18:02.250
and probably on slightly a mission

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.650
to make it something that
is talked about more.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.660
And I think some of these softer emotional

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.320
intelligence skills, like gratitude

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:11.770
and empathy and kindness

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:14.660
have been historically
associated with women.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.120
And I think that,

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:19.430
I'm an extremely alpha kind of character

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:21.880
and I think it's a little bit of an enigma

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:23.890
but I have a funny feeling I'm a preview

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:25.300
to something that is going to be far more

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.500
common in the future.

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:27.333
You know it's funny,

00:18:27.333 --> 00:18:29.160
I often publicly answer "I don't know",

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:32.490
which then leads to 500
people sending me emails

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.800
that they know, and they
would love to charge

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.877
my audience $5,000 dollars
to teach 'em, right?

00:18:37.877 --> 00:18:39.240
(chuckles)

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:40.073
Sorry.

00:18:40.073 --> 00:18:41.901
I just am thinking about that.

00:18:41.901 --> 00:18:45.800
I'm not sure, but I will say this.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:47.580
The thing that I've seen work,

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.630
the closest advice I've given
to people that work for me

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:52.254
and my friends that has worked

00:18:52.254 --> 00:18:55.120
but I'm not so sold that
it's building self-awareness,

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.140
but it might be a precursor.

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.550
Is creating relationships
with the people that

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:00.920
know you the best

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:04.703
that allows a safe place for
them to tell you the truth.

00:19:07.141 --> 00:19:09.390
And this is why I think therapy is great,

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.010
couples' therapy, all
these different things

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.330
are really powerful.

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:16.581
It's how I mitigate with my managers.

00:19:16.581 --> 00:19:18.450
You need to create an environment

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:20.680
where people can give you honest feedback.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:22.366
So, in my office,

00:19:22.366 --> 00:19:24.900
people that visit me, business associates,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.022
other people are flabbergasted by the way

00:19:27.022 --> 00:19:29.200
my employees talk to me.

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:30.520
And I'm super proud of it.

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:32.530
They think they're being disrespectful,

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:35.200
I think I've created
the right environment.

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.989
If you're comfortable
enough to tell me I suck

00:19:37.989 --> 00:19:38.850
(laughs)

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:41.020
then we're on the right path, right?

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:43.620
So, I think it's by

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:45.940
if you really want to figure it out,

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:47.310
just tactically,

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:50.960
I think a hardcore two, three hour dinner

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:52.920
with the 15 people that you know the best,

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.200
family and friends

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:55.310
where you convince them,

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:56.630
in a week I need you to come back

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:58.550
and tell me the truth to every question,

00:19:58.550 --> 00:20:00.330
and you're not gonna hurt my feelings

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:01.861
and I'm not gonna lash out.

00:20:01.861 --> 00:20:04.210
If you can ever pull that off,

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:07.620
you need actual, real candor

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.810
and feedback because
people are ideological

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:12.550
and romantic of who they are or

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:13.963
what they are going to be.

00:20:16.029 --> 00:20:19.330
And then that creates emotional responses.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:20.880
It's why I love entrepreneurship so much,

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:22.490
there's nowhere to hide.

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:27.240
It doesn't matter who your grandpappy was.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:28.097
Once you

00:20:28.097 --> 00:20:30.070
and this is what I love so much about

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:31.030
organizations like this,

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.960
I genuinely believe

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:35.410
that people that start from a lower place

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:37.980
have the advantage now
in an internet world

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.600
because they lack entitlement,

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:41.490
and they've tasted negativity

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:43.530
so that they have the
stomach for adversity.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.820
I think the abundance
in the system in America

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:48.810
and the way modern parenting is

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:50.860
around people with abundance trying to

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.210
protect from losses and failure

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.210
is not setting them up for reality

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:57.260
and I see it every day, so

00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:00.317
I'm out there, talking to organizations

00:21:00.317 --> 00:21:02.710
this week with people
that live in shelters.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:04.300
I'm like, you don't see it,

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.160
it's tough being a 42 year old white man

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:08.240
and telling you this,
but here's the truth.

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:09.560
You have the advantage.

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:11.510
If you have the internet in your pocket,

00:21:11.510 --> 00:21:13.430
and you're coming from adversity,

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:16.020
you've got a different hunger,
and a different framework

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:19.491
that is far more, far more powerful

00:21:19.491 --> 00:21:21.130
for the world we now live in.

